WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
AND WELCOME TO NES-TALGIA AND WELCOME TO NES-TALGIA AND WELCOME TO NES-TALGIA AND WELCOME TO NES-TALGIA AND WELCOME TO NES-TALGIA AND WELCOME TO NES-TALGIA AND WELCOME TO NES-TALGIA AND WELCOME TO NES-TALGIA AND WELCOME TO NES-TALGIA AND WELCOME TO NES-TALGIA AND WELCOME TO NES-TALGIA AND WELCOME TO NES-TALGIA AND WELCOME TO NES-TALGIA AND WELCOME TO NES-TALGIA AND WELCOME TO NES-TALGIA AND WELCOME TO NES-TALGIA AND WELCOME TO NES-TALGIA AND WELCOME TO NES-TALGIA AND WELCOME TO NES

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:38.460
And I'm Joe. And guys, today we have a game that I have honestly never even been excited about as a TV show.

00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:40.620
Now we have to play it as a video game.

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:42.980
What do you guys think about Wheel of Fortune?

00:00:43.340 --> 00:00:45.300
I don't hate the TV show.

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:48.160
I've never really watched it.

00:00:48.220 --> 00:00:52.160
I think I remember watching it a little bit in the 90s as a small child.

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:54.600
But beyond that, no.

00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:58.020
Yeah, I mean, I never tune in for Wheel of Fortune.

00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:01.020
But when it's on, I like trying to solve the puzzles.

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:02.340
I'm not very good at it sometimes.

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:04.500
But it's fun.

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:11.960
Yeah, and it's kind of like for me, in the scope of hugely accessible game shows on television,

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:16.880
there was The Price is Right, Family Feud, Jeopardy, and Wheel of Fortune.

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:20.140
And I think Wheel of Fortune is the lowest of those four.

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:22.160
Yeah, I'd say the same thing.

00:01:22.180 --> 00:01:23.420
I was a big Feud fan.

00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:29.620
And not so much Price is Right, but I was a big Feud fan.

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:32.180
I can't wait to play Family Feud if there is one.

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:35.140
There was definitely a Family Feud on PlayStation 1.

00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:39.140
Yeah. So we can only hope that the track record would go all the way back to NES.

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:46.700
Yeah. And I mean, Game Tech, the publisher of these games, even though Rare is making them, Game

00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:47.620
Tech is the publisher.

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:55.440
And they seem to have just like acquired the electronics rights licensing for all game shows.

00:01:55.600 --> 00:02:03.000
So anything that is on TV in the late 80s that's a game show, they have the ability to like make

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:04.080
a video game about it.

00:02:04.180 --> 00:02:05.580
So I wouldn't be surprised to see the Feud.

00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:10.620
I mean, like, were they on the same network or something or they just went around to everybody

00:02:10.620 --> 00:02:11.600
and was like, we want it?

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:17.620
That's a good question because Wheel of Fortune and Jeopardy are back to back on TV and they

00:02:17.620 --> 00:02:20.700
always have been. So it was always Jeopardy first and then Wheel of Fortune.

00:02:20.820 --> 00:02:21.960
So that makes sense here.

00:02:22.040 --> 00:02:23.300
Yeah. Same with release dates.

00:02:23.320 --> 00:02:27.200
But I know that they have the rights to like Hollywood Squares too.

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:30.640
And I don't remember that being on the same channel as Jeopardy and Wheel of Fortune.

00:02:30.940 --> 00:02:32.240
So maybe they just went around.

00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:39.080
Yeah. Who can give me an overview of Wheel of Fortune if somebody listening has never watched an

00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:45.560
episode? It's Hangman with a wheel with money on it.

00:02:47.260 --> 00:02:53.040
Yeah. The wheel decides the value that the letter you guess is worth.

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:54.560
I'm not even sure that that's correct.

00:02:54.700 --> 00:02:56.380
That's correct, right? No, that's true.

00:02:56.460 --> 00:02:57.320
Yeah. That's correct. Okay.

00:02:58.040 --> 00:03:01.900
So because like I said, when I watch it on TV, I'll just be like, oh, let's try and figure

00:03:01.900 --> 00:03:05.300
out what this word is. I don't pay attention to like the people's scores or anything like that.

00:03:05.540 --> 00:03:11.920
And when you spin that wheel, you're mostly guessing consonants because you have to pay

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:16.960
for vowels. And the thinking there is just that, yeah, vowels would just give away the puzzle.

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:19.600
So you have to pay for them to have the rights to them.

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:23.140
Yeah. So you spin the wheel.

00:03:23.380 --> 00:03:24.260
You guess a consonant.

00:03:24.280 --> 00:03:30.100
If it's part of the phrase or thing that you're trying to guess, it reveals itself.

00:03:30.380 --> 00:03:32.280
You get, let's say you spin 300.

00:03:32.700 --> 00:03:35.300
Vanna has to touch it first and then it reveals itself.

00:03:35.720 --> 00:03:37.580
Yeah. What do you think? This all just happens by itself?

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:40.060
There's not someone working really hard to get this done?

00:03:40.060 --> 00:03:45.720
I'm not sold that that's Vanna White because that's a green, like Loch Ness Monster looking

00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:49.700
person. I don't know what that was, but that was not Vanna at her best.

00:03:49.880 --> 00:03:52.140
You're right. But that was a day without makeup.

00:03:52.140 --> 00:03:53.000
I would go.

00:03:53.220 --> 00:03:57.440
I would not go so far as to call that poor girl the Loch Ness Monster.

00:03:57.780 --> 00:04:00.700
That's true. That was actually, she stood on two legs.

00:04:00.700 --> 00:04:03.820
So just theoretically, definitely not.

00:04:04.820 --> 00:04:12.580
The manual really tries to use that Vanna likeness or not likeness as a real selling point,

00:04:12.680 --> 00:04:18.020
though. I don't know if you guys read this, but it says, for the little description of the game,

00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:21.160
it says, solve the puzzle on the game board by filling in the hidden letter.

00:04:21.300 --> 00:04:27.120
Each correct guess sends our beautiful blonde hostess to the board to turn over your letters.

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:31.500
Then when you solve the puzzle, see her clap for you with an exclamation point.

00:04:32.540 --> 00:04:33.900
I'm glad that there's clapping.

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:36.460
Clapping is a big part of the show if you haven't seen it.

00:04:37.140 --> 00:04:39.920
It's just very funny that they were trying to use that as like a selling point.

00:04:40.020 --> 00:04:46.000
Like, I'm going to buy this so this, you know, pixelated Vanna White will clap for me.

00:04:46.820 --> 00:04:49.380
But Vanna was like the selling point for the show.

00:04:49.580 --> 00:04:53.440
It was like that, I guess sex appeal would have been what it was.

00:04:53.620 --> 00:04:55.420
But like that she was the selling point of the show.

00:04:55.420 --> 00:04:58.800
I don't think anybody thinks of Pat Sajak like they think of Alec Trebek.

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:03.320
I think of Pat Sajak in a very, you know, raunchy way sometimes.

00:05:04.500 --> 00:05:13.400
Okay. And just as we can give the whole scope of the game, you spin the wheel.

00:05:13.580 --> 00:05:14.620
Let's say you get $300.

00:05:15.260 --> 00:05:19.780
Then if you guess the letter S and there's two of them, you get $300 for each one.

00:05:19.780 --> 00:05:26.400
Then they show up on the board and you can choose to solve or buy a vowel or spin again.

00:05:26.760 --> 00:05:31.840
But if you get it wrong, then the next person in line gets to take over.

00:05:32.040 --> 00:05:34.140
And they could solve it right there if they wanted to.

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:36.640
And that's why you don't want to give away the obvious things.

00:05:36.900 --> 00:05:38.280
Yeah, exactly.

00:05:38.900 --> 00:05:42.260
There's a lot of strategy to Wheel of Fortune, right?

00:05:42.600 --> 00:05:44.920
Maybe not. But let's go over it.

00:05:44.920 --> 00:05:48.660
What was your guys' strategy for playing Wheel of Fortune on the NES?

00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:52.000
I'll be honest. My strategy was just to try and figure it out.

00:05:52.060 --> 00:05:53.720
I was never worried like, oh, am I going to give away?

00:05:53.780 --> 00:06:00.500
I never thought too much about like, am I going to give this away to the next person if I fill in too many letters?

00:06:00.520 --> 00:06:03.880
So I was just trying to play a pretty basic game.

00:06:03.880 --> 00:06:15.400
Yeah, it's kind of hard when you take it out of the context of actual money to play this other than just like, I'm going to play Hangman.

00:06:15.620 --> 00:06:18.180
But in the context of Wheel of Fortune.

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:23.380
And yeah, I mean, that's pretty much it for me.

00:06:25.020 --> 00:06:30.640
Okay, but there had to be like some letters that you guys guessed more often than not, right?

00:06:30.840 --> 00:06:35.020
Oh, yeah. Like R, S, T, N, you know, the basics.

00:06:35.660 --> 00:06:39.760
Yeah. Yeah, nobody's going out and picking X ever, right?

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:41.340
Yeah, X is hard to –

00:06:41.340 --> 00:06:43.580
Yeah, you're an idiot if you select X.

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:48.120
Yeah, I feel like if you think there's an X in the word, you probably know the word by that point.

00:06:48.420 --> 00:06:51.600
It has to say ilophone already or something like that.

00:06:51.640 --> 00:06:58.220
Yeah, yeah. Now, in Jeopardy, there was a QWERTY keyboard, which most people are familiar with that layout.

00:06:58.520 --> 00:07:03.780
Why do you think that they didn't have that for Wheel of Fortune where you have to scroll across each letter?

00:07:04.060 --> 00:07:06.400
I think it's because they made a bad decision.

00:07:06.500 --> 00:07:07.480
Yeah, they just made a bad decision.

00:07:08.920 --> 00:07:16.240
Because that was my biggest – well, my two biggest pet peeves of this game go hand in hand with each other.

00:07:16.240 --> 00:07:23.400
One is that when you start, you only have seven seconds to pick your letter, which seems like a lot, like seven seconds to pick a letter.

00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:27.300
But that's seven seconds to even look at the board and think of what letter did you do.

00:07:27.300 --> 00:07:35.740
And then sometimes I've ran out of time just trying to scroll from A all the way over to even like R or something because it takes so long to scroll over.

00:07:35.780 --> 00:07:36.800
It takes like half your time.

00:07:37.020 --> 00:07:43.980
If you're going to put all the letters in alphabetical order, at least make several rows of them so that it doesn't take as long to navigate.

00:07:44.560 --> 00:07:50.160
Because I – like not so much in the letter selection while you're just like guessing.

00:07:50.420 --> 00:08:03.440
But if you're going to finish the sentence or like guess or solve the puzzle, you can run out of time even if you know exactly and you're just trying to get to all the letters.

00:08:03.760 --> 00:08:05.300
Yeah, that was more the issue.

00:08:05.520 --> 00:08:10.780
Not the lack of the QWERTY keyboard, but rather the fact that it's just one row of letters.

00:08:11.460 --> 00:08:12.900
And slow scrolling.

00:08:13.980 --> 00:08:19.900
Right. It just seems strange that the same developer – like Rare was totally capable of doing it in Jeopardy.

00:08:19.980 --> 00:08:21.900
So to not do it here I think was a bit of a mistake.

00:08:21.920 --> 00:08:25.440
But there was also some things that they did in Jeopardy that they didn't do here.

00:08:25.640 --> 00:08:28.300
Like there's no character avatar for you.

00:08:28.360 --> 00:08:30.820
You're just kind of there with your nameplate.

00:08:30.960 --> 00:08:31.900
We never see you.

00:08:32.580 --> 00:08:34.000
It's a first-person game.

00:08:36.100 --> 00:08:37.660
Yeah, that's kind of lame too.

00:08:37.660 --> 00:08:44.840
If you're going to make a game show game, which I guess this is the golden age of game show games here.

00:08:44.980 --> 00:08:52.980
But if you're going to make a game show game, you got to make the presentation like really shine because that's 90% of a game show.

00:08:54.240 --> 00:08:56.580
So that was disappointing to see.

00:08:56.580 --> 00:09:06.120
And then there's also the pointless power meter put into the wheel to assess your strength.

00:09:06.400 --> 00:09:08.860
I don't know about that.

00:09:08.940 --> 00:09:10.060
I think it's fine.

00:09:10.060 --> 00:09:18.400
I think it's like you're going to have to at least prove to the player that you weren't always going to get bankrupt.

00:09:18.840 --> 00:09:24.180
Like if you landed on bankrupt and all you did was press spin, you'll be like, oh, that's a fucking joke.

00:09:24.340 --> 00:09:26.000
Like that's not fair at all.

00:09:26.060 --> 00:09:36.420
But if you give them something to input like how hard you're going to spin the wheel, maybe they can like accept that maybe that is just chance.

00:09:36.440 --> 00:09:37.260
You know what I mean?

00:09:37.260 --> 00:09:39.980
I totally agree with that philosophy, Sean.

00:09:40.080 --> 00:09:44.480
And that makes a lot of sense because you do want to have some kind of idea of control.

00:09:44.660 --> 00:09:48.780
But do you feel like it actually mattered how much strength you inputted?

00:09:48.860 --> 00:09:50.660
Like did the wheel actually spin longer?

00:09:50.820 --> 00:09:52.340
I couldn't tell. It did.

00:09:52.880 --> 00:10:04.080
But yeah. For me, it was like I would just try, you know, if I just want to get on with the game anyway, I would just use little strength just to get across the wheel as far as I can.

00:10:04.080 --> 00:10:09.380
Yeah. And, you know, your chances of landing on bankrupt or 1,000 are the same either way.

00:10:09.500 --> 00:10:14.460
It's just it's just a way to make people not throw their controller, I guess.

00:10:14.680 --> 00:10:17.820
And the computers here are pretty respectful.

00:10:17.820 --> 00:10:24.660
We had that weird thing in Jeopardy where they would just guess nonsense because realistically, what else could what else could they guess?

00:10:24.660 --> 00:10:31.680
But here they don't ever guess like letters that like they don't ever guess X or U for the most part for me.

00:10:31.780 --> 00:10:34.740
I don't know about you guys, but they seem to have like some kind of coherence.

00:10:34.880 --> 00:10:40.940
And they waited until the puzzle was actually pretty close to being solved before they would jump in and solve it.

00:10:40.940 --> 00:10:41.760
Yeah.

00:10:42.920 --> 00:10:55.460
I think there's got to be like a hierarchy that all of the AIs use is like, well, I'm going to guess like T, R, N, like those first and then they start moving down.

00:10:55.620 --> 00:11:06.680
But I think that they're very liberal with how many vowels they're going to guess because I think the AI use that a lot more often than I've seen in the show.

00:11:06.680 --> 00:11:07.600
Yeah.

00:11:07.840 --> 00:11:12.260
And there's there's also the whole like easy, medium and hard mode, too.

00:11:12.560 --> 00:11:23.320
And I think the mistake would have been to have hard mode be like as soon as a letter or two is on the board, the computer just knows the answer and solves it based on little to nothing.

00:11:23.480 --> 00:11:31.980
But instead, it seems more like just the overall luck and ability of the computer players are better when they're on hard.

00:11:32.220 --> 00:11:35.020
Yeah, it'll guess correct letters more often, I assume.

00:11:35.420 --> 00:11:38.800
Right. Rather than just like guessing the solving the puzzle quickly.

00:11:39.040 --> 00:11:43.560
But did you guys did it ever did the AI ever incorrectly solve?

00:11:43.820 --> 00:11:44.740
That didn't happen. No.

00:11:45.000 --> 00:11:46.740
Whenever they solve, they get it right.

00:11:46.940 --> 00:11:52.940
Like I've played a few of these games where I didn't win, obviously.

00:11:53.280 --> 00:12:00.580
And whenever it gets to like that bonus round where we'll get to all the fun things about that.

00:12:00.580 --> 00:12:05.520
But it doesn't matter how few letters they actually reveal.

00:12:05.780 --> 00:12:11.840
Like if you just had like an M and an N, like they can immediately guess like, oh, commander in chief.

00:12:12.020 --> 00:12:14.720
It's like, well, nobody was going to get that.

00:12:14.860 --> 00:12:16.080
But OK, whatever.

00:12:16.340 --> 00:12:17.660
I'm already out of the game.

00:12:17.660 --> 00:12:18.740
So I guess it doesn't matter.

00:12:19.560 --> 00:12:46.380
Yeah. And so, Sean, you mentioned that there are some other rounds.

00:12:46.380 --> 00:12:49.860
It's the same as a normal Wheel of Fortune format on the TV show.

00:12:50.000 --> 00:12:52.800
You have two regular rounds where everybody plays.

00:12:52.800 --> 00:12:56.840
You spin until you guess a letter wrong or go bankrupt or miss a turn.

00:12:56.840 --> 00:13:06.760
And then in the third round is the speed round where if you guess a letter, you have to pretty much either solve or pass your turn.

00:13:07.060 --> 00:13:10.740
Did you guys think that that made it like a little more challenging?

00:13:10.860 --> 00:13:13.240
Did it add anything to the game for you?

00:13:13.400 --> 00:13:24.800
For me, it was kind of just like an experience in patience because it would be several rounds before we revealed enough letters for anyone to know what it was.

00:13:26.060 --> 00:13:28.380
I mean, it definitely changes the dynamic.

00:13:29.140 --> 00:13:37.900
And again, I'm in quarantine, so I don't know if I could even play this with another person.

00:13:38.080 --> 00:13:44.760
But I feel like this game would be a lot more interesting if it were with non-computers.

00:13:44.760 --> 00:13:54.700
So I think that's what sort of harms the third round because like in the previous rounds, you could kind of like dominate as long as you're guessing well.

00:13:55.460 --> 00:14:00.520
Whereas in this one, you have to like there's no way you're going to get this in your first or second guess.

00:14:00.700 --> 00:14:02.520
So you have to pass it.

00:14:02.520 --> 00:14:11.220
So I played this with my girlfriend actually and I think that it was fine playing with two players.

00:14:11.440 --> 00:14:14.400
I think because like you said, there's no actual money involved.

00:14:14.960 --> 00:14:17.700
It kind of just feels like maybe just play Hangman.

00:14:18.540 --> 00:14:19.960
Like it was fine.

00:14:19.960 --> 00:14:26.060
It was probably better, I assume, better than playing against the computer just because there's that like real competition.

00:14:26.060 --> 00:14:31.520
There was one game where I had solved more puzzles, but she won because she had more money.

00:14:32.100 --> 00:14:37.840
And I guess that's just like a preferences to the gameplay, but it's just, I don't know.

00:14:38.100 --> 00:14:45.560
I think that it's really, there is no, nothing that it being a video game brings to Hangman.

00:14:46.140 --> 00:14:54.880
Like, you know, like unless you are giving out real money, don't play Wheel of Fortune is like with another person, you know.

00:14:55.720 --> 00:14:58.700
What it would bring as a video game is being able to play by yourself.

00:14:59.000 --> 00:15:03.820
Yeah, Joe, I think what you just touched upon is a dilemma that I like to call the Texas Hold'em dilemma,

00:15:03.820 --> 00:15:09.780
where if there's no money involved, it just like sooner or later, someone's just going to be like,

00:15:10.120 --> 00:15:13.940
well, I'm all in because they don't actually care about what's at stake.

00:15:13.940 --> 00:15:15.100
Yeah, it's just chips.

00:15:15.560 --> 00:15:24.880
Exactly. Like that's like, I actually did just have a, I played a game of Texas Hold'em where we were just playing with chips that had nothing behind them.

00:15:25.900 --> 00:15:27.600
And that's, that's pretty much it.

00:15:27.660 --> 00:15:33.960
Whenever you have like a game like this, where there's a dollar value, like, I guess you could sort of count it as like,

00:15:34.040 --> 00:15:38.860
oh, I got the high score, but like you're definitely not going to be playing the game the same way.

00:15:38.860 --> 00:15:47.320
And it kind of ruins like the, like the fundamentals of how, of how the game is supposed to be played because nobody actually gives a shit.

00:15:47.520 --> 00:15:48.880
So, I don't know.

00:15:49.020 --> 00:15:56.300
Yeah. And if the dollar value, I mean, if it were something that there was some strategy to getting a certain dollar value or, you know,

00:15:56.360 --> 00:16:02.100
doing this will maybe get you more money, but at a higher risk or something, then I can see it being like, that's gamified.

00:16:02.100 --> 00:16:08.240
But when it's just, I mean, maybe this is a fundamental problem with Wheel of Fortune, but when it's just been a wheel and you get a random number,

00:16:09.260 --> 00:16:12.320
that's, it's flashier, but it's not, to me, not as fun.

00:16:12.480 --> 00:16:21.620
And that also like moves into the bonus round where you're playing for a prize where you can either get a Porsche,

00:16:21.960 --> 00:16:27.300
which is actually like product placement for Porsches or Porsche, I don't know how to pronounce it.

00:16:27.300 --> 00:16:33.160
Um, or you can go to Hawaii or you could get a, like a whole new kitchen renovation.

00:16:34.420 --> 00:16:40.240
And like, after you play the bonus round, if you win, it just shows that, okay, you won that.

00:16:40.440 --> 00:16:50.240
Like it would have been like, there would have been some kind of like reward if they kind of like showed you driving away in your Porsche or something.

00:16:50.460 --> 00:16:51.360
Like that would be cool.

00:16:51.540 --> 00:16:55.320
Sean, they needed to, they needed to show animation, but even better.

00:16:55.320 --> 00:17:02.820
Imagine if these unlocked like little mini games of like driving or like doing something, surfing on an island.

00:17:03.240 --> 00:17:08.720
Yeah. Right. Just something as like a mini game as a reason to want to win all of the prizes.

00:17:08.880 --> 00:17:12.500
But instead who selects like, no, I don't want the Porsche.

00:17:12.500 --> 00:17:14.040
Give me the deluxe kitchen.

00:17:14.200 --> 00:17:15.820
And I don't even give a fuck if I get either.

00:17:16.200 --> 00:17:22.640
Yeah. Cause all it does is it shows you the same logo for the, for the item, but it puts like flashy lights around it.

00:17:22.640 --> 00:17:26.900
Like you guys don't get excited about the idea of imagining you're going to go to Hawaii.

00:17:27.440 --> 00:17:37.800
